  "problem": "The following table shows the number of families in a town that have a dog and the number of families that do not.\n\n| Families who have a dog | Families that do not have a dog |\n| --- | --- |\n| $151$ | $92$ |\n\nRounding to the nearest ten, find the approximate ratio of the number of families that have a dog to those that do not.",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's approximate the number of families who have a dog and those who do not by rounding to the nearest $10$:\n\n- Families who have a dog: $151⟶150$\n\n- Families who do not have a dog: $92⟶90$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the number of families who have a dog to those who do not is approximately:\n\n$$\n\\frac{150}{90}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $10$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{150÷10}{90÷10}=\\frac{15}{9}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{15÷3}{9÷3}=\\frac{5}{3}\n$$\n\nHence, the ratio of the number of families who have a dog and who do not have is approximately $5:3.$",
